# Python tooling: use uv

This project is managed with [uv](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv). Use `uv` for dependencies, virtual environments, and running commands — not `pip`, `pipenv`, `poetry`, or `python -m venv` unless the user explicitly asks otherwise.

## Commands to use

| Task | Command |
|------|---------|
| Install / sync deps | `uv sync` |
| CI / reproducible install | `uv sync --frozen` |
| Run a script or tool | `uv run <command>` |
| Run tests | `uv run pytest` |
| Add a dependency | `uv add <package>` |
| Add a dev dependency | `uv add --group dev <package>` |
| Remove a dependency | `uv remove <package>` |
| Update lock file | `uv lock` |
| Create venv (if needed) | `uv venv` |

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD
pip install pytest
python -m venv .venv
poetry add requests

# ✅ GOOD
uv sync
uv add requests
uv run pytest
uv run uvicorn src.routes.app:app --reload --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

## Notes

- Prefer `uv sync` over `uv pip install` — the lock file is `uv.lock`.
- Use `uv run` so commands use the project environment without manual activation.
- After changing `pyproject.toml` dependencies, run `uv lock` (if needed) and `uv sync`.
